course: dictionary
lesson: 6425
title: Mutualism: when both sides gain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mutualism is a relationship between two different kinds of living things in which both of them benefit. Benefit means gain something good. Look for the clue in the sentence: «Banded mongooses pick ticks off warthogs lying down; a friendship where both sides gain is called mutualism.» The mongooses get food, and the warthogs get rid of ticks, so both sides gain.
explain: The word mutual means shared, or done by both sides. So mutualism is a sharing relationship: each partner gives something and gets something back. Bees and flowers work this way. The bee gets nectar to eat, and the flower gets its pollen carried to other flowers.
explain: Mutualism is one kind of symbiosis, which means living things living closely together. Two other kinds are different. In parasitism, one living thing is harmed. In commensalism, one benefits and the other is not affected. Check the sentence: if both gain, it is mutualism.
fact: Mutualism is a relationship between two different living things in which both benefit.
fact: Examples of mutualism are bees and flowers, and clownfish and sea anemones.
fact: «Birds such as cattle tyrants and yellow-headed caracaras ride on capybaras and eat ticks and flies; both gain, which scientists call mutualism.» The birds get food, and the capybara gets rid of pests.
fact: Mutualism is a noun; the adjective is mutualistic, as in «a mutualistic relationship».
fact: Parasitism means one living thing is harmed. Commensalism means one benefits and the other is not affected.
exercise: «Banded mongooses pick ticks off warthogs lying down; a friendship where both sides gain is called mutualism.» What is mutualism? | *A relationship where both living things benefit | A relationship where neither living thing is ever near the other | A relationship where one living thing is harmed
exercise: Which of these is an example of mutualism? | A leaf falling onto a rock | *Bees and flowers | A tick sucking blood from a dog
exercise: «Both gain, which scientists call mutualism.» In a sentence like this, which relationship harms one of the living things? | *Parasitism | Mutualism | Commensalism
test: Clownfish and sea anemones live together, and both get something good from it. What is this relationship called? | Commensalism | Parasitism | *Mutualism
test: In a mutualistic relationship, what happens? | One living thing is harmed | *Both living things gain something | Only one living thing gains
exam: A mongoose eats ticks off a warthog, and the warthog is left cleaner. Is this mutualism, and why? | No, because the warthog is harmed | No, because only the mongoose gains | *Yes, because both animals gain | No, because animals are not living things
